#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# power_mod

#% text
# `power_mod(a, b, m)` gives `mod(a^b, m)`, computed without forming `a^b`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A negative exponent uses the inverse of `a` modulo `m`, which must exist.

#% item
# The exponent may be a large integer; the work is proportional to its number of bits.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
power_mod(2, 10, 1000)
#% output
#= 24

#% cell
power_mod(3, 200, 7)
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
power_mod(3, -1, 7)
#% output
#= 5

#% cell
power_mod(2, 10^20, 10^9 + 7)
#% output
#= 855473248

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The Fermat test of 561, a Carmichael number, which passes for every base prime to it:

#% cell
power_mod(2, 560, 561)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
is_prime(561)
#% output
#= false

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The same as reducing the full power:

#% cell
[power_mod(7, 13, 50), mod(7^13, 50)]
#% output
#= [7, 7]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# An inverse that does not exist:

#% cell
power_mod(2, -1, 8)
#% output
#= power_mod(2, -1, 8)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `mod`, `modular_inverse`, `multiplicative_order`, `primitive_root`
